# Naming, Clean Code & SOLID

## Naming — camelCase

Use **camelCase** for variables, functions, methods, and object properties in application code (TypeScript/JavaScript).

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const document_id = id;
function Process_Image(raw_data: Buffer) {}

// ✅ GOOD
const documentId = id;
function processImage(rawData: Buffer) {}
```

- Types/classes: `PascalCase`
- Files/folders: match repo convention; prefer `camelCase` or `kebab-case` consistently — **identifiers inside code stay camelCase**
- Env vars / AWS resource names in CDK: follow AWS/CDK norms (`UPPER_SNAKE` for env keys is OK)

## Clean Code

- Small functions with one responsibility; names reveal intent
- No dead code, no commented-out blocks, no speculative abstractions
- Prefer early returns over deep nesting
- Explicit errors; never empty `catch`
- Keep modules cohesive; avoid god handlers — extract use-cases/services

## SOLID (always)

| Principle | Practice here |
|-----------|----------------|
| **S** | One reason to change per module (e.g. `presignUploads` ≠ `runOcr`) |
| **O** | Extend via new handlers/strategies, not endless `if` chains on type/status |
| **L** | Implementations must honor their port/interface contracts |
| **I** | Narrow ports (`DocumentRepository`, `OcrEngine`) — no fat “AwsClient” bags |
| **D** | Depend on ports; inject Textract/Dynamo/S3 adapters at the edges |

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — handler knows Textract + Dynamo + domain mixed
export async function handler(event: SQSEvent) {
  const textract = new TextractClient({});
  // parse, OCR, map, persist all inline...
}

// ✅ GOOD — handler orchestrates; domain depends on ports
export async function handler(event: SQSEvent) {
  await processDocumentMessage(event, { ocr, documents, clock });
}
```

Lambda entrypoints stay thin: parse event → call use case → map result/errors.
